# MedTech Market Performance — AI Agent

A free, open-source chat agent that answers market-share questions grounded in the same
governed dataset as this project's Power BI model and Databricks pipeline — built directly on
top of the [AI Prompting Pack](../03_AI_Prompting_Pack/AI_Prompting_Pack.docx)'s structured
prompt template.

Ask it things like:
- *"Why did market share change year over year?"*
- *"Which products contributed most to the share change?"*
- *"What are the top 10 brands this year?"*
- *"Can these numbers be trusted?"*

## Why this is safe to ship as a public demo

Every number the agent reports comes from one of six deterministic Python functions
(`tools/data_tools.py`) — the LLM never writes or runs its own query. Each function encodes
one specific, auditable business definition (the same ones as the DAX measures and PySpark
functions elsewhere in this project), reading only from a pre-exported, already-governed
snapshot of the Gold dataset (`data/*.parquet` — already deduplicated, already uniquely
mapped, ambiguous/inactive products already excluded). That means:

- The agent **cannot** query raw or quarantined data, because it has no tool that exposes it.
- The agent **cannot** silently invent a number, because every figure traces to a named tool
  call — shown to the user via the "Show tool calls" toggle in the sidebar.
- A hard-coded prohibition in the system prompt (`prompts/system_prompt.md`) stops it from
  inventing a *cause* for a change — contribution (pp) is explicitly framed as "which," not "why."

`tests/test_tools_reconcile.py` proves the tools themselves are correct — it reconciles every
control total live and checks the additive contribution property — independent of which LLM
is answering on top of them.

## Architecture

```
Person's question
      |
      v
Streamlit chat UI (app.py)
      |
      v
agent.py -- tool-calling loop (Anthropic or Groq, your choice)
      |
      v
tools/data_tools.py -- six deterministic functions, e.g. get_market_share_summary()
      |
      v
data/*.parquet -- pre-exported, already-governed Gold snapshot (no live DB connection needed)
```

## Run it locally

```bash
cd ai_agent
pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run app.py
```

Open the URL Streamlit prints (usually `http://localhost:8501`), pick a provider in the
sidebar, paste in your own API key, and start asking questions.

### Getting a free API key

| Provider | Free tier | Get a key |
|---|---|---|
| **Groq** (recommended) | Generous free rate limits, no payment method required, fast open-weight models (Llama 3.1) | [console.groq.com/keys](https://console.groq.com/keys) |
| **Anthropic** | Free trial credits for new accounts | [console.anthropic.com](https://console.anthropic.com/) |

Your key is only ever sent directly to that provider's own API from your browser session —
this app never stores, logs, or transmits it anywhere else, and it's never committed to the
repo (see `.gitignore`).

## Host it for free (so anyone visiting your GitHub repo can try it live)

### Option A — Streamlit Community Cloud (simplest, recommended)
1. Push this repo to GitHub (public).
2. Go to [share.streamlit.io](https://share.streamlit.io) → **New app**.
3. Point it at your repo, branch, and set the main file path to `ai_agent/app.py`.
4. Deploy. It rebuilds automatically on every push. Free tier is sufficient for a personal
   project — the app itself costs nothing to run since visitors bring their own API key.
5. Add the live URL to your repo's README / GitHub "About" section so it's one click away.

### Option B — Hugging Face Spaces
1. Create a new Space → SDK: **Streamlit** (or **Docker**, using the included `Dockerfile`).
2. Push this folder's contents to the Space's repo (or link your GitHub repo).
3. Space builds and hosts it automatically, free, at `huggingface.co/spaces/<you>/<space-name>`.

Both options are genuinely $0/month for a project at this scale — no server to pay for, no
database, no LLM cost to you as the repo owner.

## Extending it

- Add a new capability by writing a new function in `tools/data_tools.py`, adding its schema
  to `tools/schema.py`, and registering it in `TOOL_FUNCTIONS` — the agent picks it up
  automatically, no changes needed in `agent.py`.
- To point this at a *live* Databricks Gold table instead of the bundled parquet snapshot,
  replace the `_load()` function in `data_tools.py` with a call to `databricks-sql-connector`
  or a Delta Sharing client — every tool function downstream is unaffected since they only
  depend on the resulting pandas DataFrame shape.

